{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Steering File Audit",
  "description": "Project-agnostic holistic evaluation of all steering files in .kiro/steering/. Identifies gaps, ambiguities, internal inconsistencies, cross-file conflicts, inclusion mode optimization opportunities, and token inefficiencies. Respects file prefix conventions: core-/domain- files are immutable org standards, se- files are reusable team standards that must not contain project-specific content, and all other files are project-specific.",
  "version": "2",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Perform a comprehensive audit of all steering files in .kiro/steering/. Read every file in that directory. Then evaluate them across the following dimensions, presenting findings grouped by category. After presenting findings, interview the user about any gaps or ambiguities that need clarification before proposing changes.\n\nIMPORTANT: This audit is project-agnostic. Do not assume any particular project structure, tech stack, or file naming. Discover everything from the steering files themselves.\n\n## Step 0 — Discovery\n\nBefore auditing, build a mental model of the project by reading all steering files:\n- Identify which files describe the tech stack, product, structure, and workflows\n- Catalog every technology, framework, language, tool, and pattern mentioned\n- Note the inclusion mode (always, auto, fileMatch, manual) of each file from its front-matter\n- Classify each file by its prefix: core-, domain-, se-, or project-specific (no prefix or any prefix not listed)\n\n## File Prefix Conventions\n\nApply these rules when evaluating conflicts and content placement:\n\n- **core-** and **domain-** prefixed files are IMMUTABLE org-level standards. When a project-level file conflicts with a core-/domain- file, the project-level file is the one that must change (or document an explicit, justified override).\n- **se-** prefixed files are reusable team-level standards that must NOT contain project-specific content. They should be portable across any project using the same technology. If you find project-specific details (specific repo names, database/collection names, service names, account IDs, resource ARNs, URLs unique to one project, or instructions that only apply to one particular codebase) in an se- file, flag it.\n- All other files (any file without a core-, domain-, or se- prefix) are project-specific and may contain project-specific instructions freely.\n\n## Audit Dimensions\n\n### 1. Cross-File Conflicts\nIdentify statements that contradict each other across files. Pay special attention to:\n- Conflicts between core-/domain- (immutable) files and project-level files — the project file must yield\n- Conflicts between se- files and project-specific files\n- Contradictory technology recommendations (e.g., one file recommends framework X, another recommends framework Y for the same purpose)\n- Contradictory process instructions (e.g., different commit message formats, different test commands, different hook strategies)\n- Contradictory security, auth, or logging patterns\n\nFor each conflict, state: which files conflict, quote the specific contradictory statements, and state which file takes precedence per the prefix rules.\n\n### 2. Internal Ambiguities\nWithin individual files, identify:\n- Vague instructions that could be interpreted multiple ways\n- TODO/placeholder sections that are incomplete (especially problematic in core-/domain- files since they are immutable — incomplete standards create confusion)\n- Rules that lack enough specificity to be actionable\n- Inconsistent use of \"should\" vs \"must\" vs \"recommended\" — these imply different enforcement levels\n\n### 3. Content Duplication\nIdentify content that is substantively repeated across multiple files. For each duplication:\n- State which files contain the overlapping content\n- Assess whether the duplication is harmful (risk of drift if one copy is updated but not the other) or acceptable (necessary local context)\n- Suggest which file should be the single source of truth, and whether the other file(s) should reference it or remove the duplicate\n\n### 4. Gaps — Interview the User\nBased on the tech stack and workflows discovered in Step 0, identify areas where steering is missing or insufficient. Consider:\n- Technologies in active use that have no corresponding standards file\n- Workflows mentioned in one file but not documented anywhere\n- Security, auth, or infrastructure patterns in use but not covered by any steering\n- Missing guidance for common concerns: error handling, API design, testing strategy, database access patterns, deployment, monitoring\n- Cross-cutting concerns that fall between files with no clear owner\n\nFor each gap, ask the user a specific question to determine if steering is needed and what it should say. Do not assume — interview.\n\n### 5. Inclusion Mode Optimization\nEvaluate whether each file's inclusion mode is optimal for minimizing unnecessary token consumption:\n- Files included as \"always\" or \"auto\" that are only relevant when working with specific file types or technologies — should they use fileMatch instead?\n- Files with fileMatch patterns that are too broad (matching files they are not relevant to) or too narrow (missing files they should apply to)\n- Large files that could save significant tokens by switching from always/auto to fileMatch or manual\n- Files that are manual but apply so frequently they should be auto or always\n- Files with no front-matter at all (defaulting to always) — is that intentional?\n\nFor each suggestion, note the approximate file size and estimate the token impact of unnecessary inclusions.\n\n### 6. Token Efficiency\nIdentify opportunities to reduce token consumption without losing behavioral value:\n- Verbose sections that could be condensed while preserving the same instructions\n- Large code examples where a shorter example would teach the same point\n- Anti-pattern tables that substantially repeat rules already stated in prose above them\n- Reference links pointing to files that do not exist in the repo (dead links waste tokens and may confuse the agent into searching for them)\n- Sections that are mostly TODO/placeholder with no actionable content — these consume tokens while providing no steering value\n- Repeated boilerplate across files (e.g., identical metadata blocks, identical reference sections)\n\nFor each finding, estimate the approximate token savings and confirm the change would not regress the file's behavioral intent.\n\n### 7. se- File Purity Check\nFor each se- prefixed file, verify it contains NO project-specific content. Flag any:\n- References to specific database names, collection names, table names, or schema details belonging to one project\n- Specific service names, container names, or repository paths that are unique to one codebase\n- Specific account IDs, resource ARNs, URLs, or credentials references unique to one deployment\n- Instructions that only make sense in the context of one particular project rather than being reusable across any project using the same technology stack\n\nFor each violation, quote the project-specific content and suggest whether it should be moved to a project-specific file or generalized.\n\n## Output Format\n\nPresent findings in this structure:\n\n```\nSTEERING AUDIT REPORT\n\nFiles analyzed: [count]\nClassification: [N] core/domain (immutable) | [N] se- (team reusable) | [N] project-specific\n\n═══ CONFLICTS (X found) ═══\n[numbered list with file names, quoted contradictions, and precedence ruling]\n\n═══ AMBIGUITIES (X found) ═══\n[numbered list with file name, quoted text, and what is unclear]\n\n═══ DUPLICATIONS (X found) ═══\n[numbered list with file names, what is duplicated, and recommended single source of truth]\n\n═══ GAPS — QUESTIONS FOR YOU ═══\n[numbered questions about missing steering, each with context on why it matters]\n\n═══ INCLUSION OPTIMIZATION (X suggestions) ═══\n[numbered list with file name, current mode, suggested mode, and estimated token impact]\n\n═══ TOKEN EFFICIENCY (X suggestions) ═══\n[numbered list with file name, section, suggestion, and estimated token savings]\n\n═══ se- PURITY VIOLATIONS (X found) ═══\n[numbered list with file name, quoted project-specific content, and suggestion]\n```\n\nAfter presenting the report, ask the user which findings they want to address. Do NOT make any file changes without explicit user approval. For gaps, conduct a brief interview to gather the user's intent before proposing content."
  }
}